<p>Reading is unnatural. This will hardly come as a surprise to any child trying to put off his homework for just one more hour of watching other kids play Minecraft. Yet there really are implications worth considering if we accept that our cognitive systems didn’t evolve to process text in the same way as they evolved to handle vision and speech. <span style="font-size: 13pt;">Speaking on an evolutionary historical scale, “we haven’t had </span><span style="font-size: 13pt;">enough time to develop a dedicated reading brain,” </span>says <a href="https://seis.ucla.edu/faculty/rebecca-gotlieb/">UCLA neuroscientist Rebecca Gotlieb</a> in <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X1L1Hd3xfrU">the BBC World Service video above</a>. For the time being, in a kind of cognitive jerry-rigging, “we co-opt parts of the brain involved in vision and auditory processing and language and attention and affect.”</p>
<p>That was true for readers and writers of Sumerian cuneiform and Egyptian hieroglyphics five millennia ago. It still is for readers and writers of the many scripts in use today, from the Latin alphabet to <a href="https://www.openculture.com/?p=1122913">Chinese characters</a> to the Arabic abjad, each of which has its own influence on its users’ neurological architecture. <span style="font-size: 13pt;">One of the biggest differences between then and now is the scale of reading: ancient Sumeria and Egypt seem to have had literacy rates around one percent, a number comprising mostly scribes, priests, and nobles, while the global average today comes in around 88 percent and rising. Another is the medium of reading: the shift from papyrus to paper may have seemed remarkable at the time, but the more recent one from paper to electronic screens has already changed the way we think about our world.</span></p>
