<p>“When we read on screens, we tend to skim, and when we skim, we’re more susceptible to misinformation,” says Gotlieb. So we’ve been told, many times and many ways, amid the past decade’s ever-more-troubled political reportage. In 2018, Maryanne Wolf, author of <a href="https://amzn.to/4yIXiCR"><em>Come Home: The Reading Brain in a Digital World</em>,</a> argued in the&nbsp;<em>Guardian&nbsp;</em>that the&nbsp;“subtle atrophy of critical analysis and empathy” apparently caused by technology-encouraged&nbsp;skimming “affects our ability to navigate a constant bombardment of information. It incentivizes a retreat to the most familiar silos of unchecked information, which require and receive no analysis, leaving us susceptible to false information and demagoguery.”</p>
<p>So much for us adults. What about the children? In the BBC World Service video, Wolf bluntly sums up the results of studies so far on the effects of early reading experience on later intellectual performance:&nbsp;“the more digital, the worse academic.”&nbsp;If a young brain is “constantly being distracted and hyperstimulated, you’re going to have them not able to really move from one stimulus to the next without a desire for ever-quicker intervals between stimuli.” For our part, as parents, teachers, or other visible examples of grown-uphood, we can do nothing to counteract that more effectively than to model and enjoy a rich “reading life” for the next generation. We must, in other words, <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2019/07/how-to-read-many-more-books-in-a-year.html">pick up books</a> and not put them down, which may strike an Open Culture reader as an easy task. But ask yourself this: <span style="font-size: 13pt;">how many times did you click over to something else before getting to the end of this post?</span></p>
